ing an accumulator pattern with a loop and a standard for loop with a counter. So I can\'t use a while loop with a counter, but I need to use a for loop with an explicit counter.\n\nLet me think about the steps again.\n\nFirst, for each number i from 1 to n:\n\n- Compute the sum of its proper divisors, called s.\n- If s is <=n and s != i, then check if the sum of s\'s proper divisors is equal to i.\n- If so, then add both i and s to the result.\n\nBut how to compute the sum of proper divisors?\n\nThe proper divisors of a number are all divisors except the number itself. So for example, 6 has divisors 1, 2, 3.\n\nSo the function to compute the sum of proper divisors:\n\ndef sum_proper_divisors(n):\n    if n == 1:\n        return 0\n    total = 1  # 1 is a proper divisor of all numbers >1\n    if n == 2:\n        return 1\n    sqrt_n = int(math.sqrt(n))\n    for i in range(2, sqrt_n + 1):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            total += i\n            other = n // i\n            if other != i:\n                total += other\n    return total\n\nWait, but this would include 1 as a divisor. So for example, for 6, the sum is 1+2+3=6. But the proper divisors of 6 are 1, 2, 3. So the sum is 6. So that\'s correct.\n\nBut then, for each number i, we calculate s = sum of proper divisors. Then, if s is <=n and s !=i, and sum_proper_divisors(s) ==i, then add both i and s to the result.\n\nBut how to handle this in code.\n\nBut the problem requires that the function is written in Python, and the code must follow the rules: use an accumulator pattern with a loop, and use a standard for loop with a counter.\n\nSo, the steps:\n\nInitialize a variable total_sum to 0.\n\nLoop through each number i from 1 to n:\n\n- Compute s = sum_proper_divisors(i)\n- If s is <=n and s !=i:\n   - Check if sum_proper_divisors(s) ==i\n   - If yes, add i and s to total_sum.\n\nBut wait, if i and s are part of an amicable pair, then both should be added. However, if i is part of a pair, but s is not in th