n with a loop:\n\ndef count(boolean_list):\n    count = 0\n    for value in boolean_list:\n        if value:\n            count += 1\n    return count\n\nBut the sum method is more concise. However, the user specified to use an accumulator pattern with a loop. So perhaps the loop approach is required.\n\nBut the problem says "the solution must be written imperatively, as if you were writing it in C or Java." In Python, even with sum, it\'s a functional approach, but maybe the user expects a loop. So I should provide both approaches? But the user wants a single solution.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the user is okay with the sum method as it\'s more efficient. But the problem says to use an accumulator pattern with a loop. So perhaps the loop approach is required.\n\nTherefore, the code should use a loop to count the Trues. So the function would be:\n\ndef count(boolean_list):\n    count = 0\n    for value in boolean_list:\n        if value:\n            count += 1\n    return count\n\nBut the user might also want to use the sum function. However, the problem states to use an accumulator pattern with a loop, so the loop is required.\n\nSo the final code would be the loop version. Let me check the example given in the problem. The problem says the function should accept a list of booleans and return the count. The example in the problem\'s file is just a description, but the actual code needs to be written.\n\nTherefore, the correct code is the loop version. The user might have intended for the loop approach, so I should go with that.\n</think>\n\n<answer>\ndef count(boolean_list):\n    count = 0\n    for value in boolean_list:\n        if value:\n            count += 1\n    return count\n</answer>'
