that appears most frequently." So if there\'s a tie, which character do we remove? The problem doesn\'t specify, so perhaps the function should remove all characters that have the maximum frequency, regardless of which one. But in code, how to handle that?\n\nIn Python, I can use a dictionary to count the frequencies. Then find the maximum frequency value. Then, iterate through the string and remove all characters that have that frequency. But if multiple characters have the same max frequency, all of them are removed.\n\nSo, steps in code:\n\n- Count the frequency of each character in the input string.\n- Find the maximum frequency value.\n- Collect all characters that have this maximum frequency.\n- Remove all of these characters from the string.\n- Return the length of the resulting string.\n\nNow, implementing this in Python. Let\'s think about the code.\n\nBut the problem says to put all logic into a single large function. Also, avoid helper functions. So the function minimum_Length should handle all this.\n\nLet\'s write the function.\n\nFirst, the input is a string. So:\n\ndef minimum_Length(s: str) -> int:\n    # Count frequencies\n    freq = {}\n    for char in s:\n        if char in freq:\n            freq[char] += 1\n        else:\n            freq[char] = 1\n    # Find max frequency\n    max_freq = max(freq.values())\n    # Collect all characters with max frequency\n    chars_to_remove = [char for char, count in freq.items() if count == max_freq]\n    # Remove all of them from the string\n    new_s = \'\'.join([c for c in s if c not in chars_to_remove])\n    return len(new_s)\n\nWait, but in Python, if there are multiple characters with the same max frequency, all of them are removed. So for example, if the string is \'aabbc\', then the frequencies are a:2, b:2, c:1. Max is 2. So chars_to_remove are a and b. Then new_s is \'c\', length 1. Which is correct.\n\nAnother example: \'aabbcc\' would have max frequency 2. chars_to_remove are a, b, c. So new_s is